{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 15,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.1,
            0.104,
            0.137,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "128"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.782,
            0.105,
            0.899,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "MATHEMATICS"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.138,
            0.408,
            0.158
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "8.4 Trigonometric Identities"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.165,
            0.595,
            0.263
          ],
          "content": "You may recall that an equation is called an identity when it is true for all values of the variables involved. Similarly, an equation involving trigonometric ratios of an angle is called a trigonometric identity, if it is true for all values of the angle(s) involved."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.268,
            0.594,
            0.326
          ],
          "content": "In this section, we will prove one trigonometric identity, and use it further to prove other useful trigonometric identities."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.698,
            0.138,
            0.884,
            0.299
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.742,
                0.302,
                0.84,
                0.321
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 8.21"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.332,
            0.665,
            0.352
          ],
          "content": "In $\\Delta   \\mathrm { A B C } ,$ , right-angled at B (see Fig. 8.21), we have:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.355,
            0.356,
            0.897,
            0.376
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{AB^{2}+BC^{2}=AC^{2}}\\tag{1}",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.382,
            0.549,
            0.402
          ],
          "content": "Dividing each term of (1) by $\\mathbf { A } \\mathbf { C } ^ { 2 } ,$ , we get"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.342,
            0.414,
            0.55,
            0.458
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{\\mathrm{AB}^{2}}{\\mathrm{AC}^{2}}+\\frac{\\mathrm{BC}^{2}}{\\mathrm{AC}^{2}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{AC}^{2}}{\\mathrm{AC}^{2}}",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.485,
            0.139,
            0.503
          ],
          "content": "i.e.,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.305,
            0.467,
            0.569,
            0.513
          ],
          "content": "\\left( \\frac{AB}{AC} \\right)^{2} + \\left( \\frac{BC}{AC} \\right)^{2} = \\left( \\frac{AC}{AC} \\right)^{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.525,
            0.138,
            0.543
          ],
          "content": "i.e.,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.285,
            0.524,
            0.512,
            0.544
          ],
          "content": "\\left( \\cos A \\right)^{2} + \\left( \\sin A \\right)^{2} = 1",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.552,
            0.139,
            0.57
          ],
          "content": "i.e.,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.303,
            0.551,
            0.897,
            0.57
          ],
          "content": "\\cos ^{2}\\mathbf{A}+\\sin ^{2}\\mathbf{A}=\\mathbf{1}\\tag{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.586,
            0.898,
            0.631
          ],
          "content": "This is true for all A such that $0 ^ { \\circ } \\leq \\mathrm { A } \\leq 9 0 ^ { \\circ }$ . So, this is a trigonometric identity. Let us now divide (1) by $\\mathbf { A B } ^ { 2 } ,$ We get"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.34,
            0.643,
            0.549,
            0.688
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{\\mathrm{AB}^{2}}{\\mathrm{AB}^{2}}+\\frac{\\mathrm{BC}^{2}}{\\mathrm{AB}^{2}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{AC}^{2}}{\\mathrm{AB}^{2}}",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_18.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.716,
            0.128,
            0.731
          ],
          "content": "or,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.306,
            0.696,
            0.57,
            0.742
          ],
          "content": "\\left( \\frac{\\mathrm{AB}}{\\mathrm{AB}} \\right)^{2} + \\left( \\frac{\\mathrm{BC}}{\\mathrm{AB}} \\right)^{2} = \\left( \\frac{\\mathrm{AC}}{\\mathrm{AB}} \\right)^{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_20.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.754,
            0.139,
            0.771
          ],
          "content": "i.e.,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.36,
            0.752,
            0.897,
            0.771
          ],
          "content": "\\mathbf{1} + \\tan^{2} \\mathbf{A} = \\sec^{2} \\mathbf{A}\\tag{3}",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_22.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.777,
            0.899,
            0.816
          ],
          "content": "Is this equation true for $\\mathrm { A   =   0 ^ { \\circ } ? }$ Yes, it is. What about $\\mathrm { A }   =   9 0 ^ { \\circ } ?$ Well, tan A and sec A are not defined for $A = 90^{\\circ}.\\  SO ,(3)$ is true for all A such that $0 ^ { \\circ } \\leq \\mathrm { A } < 9 0 ^ { \\circ }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.822,
            0.679,
            0.842
          ],
          "content": "Let us see what we get on dividing (1) by BC². We get"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.342,
            0.851,
            0.551,
            0.897
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{AB^{2}}{BC^{2}}+\\frac{BC^{2}}{BC^{2}}=\\frac{AC^{2}}{BC^{2}}",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_25.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.967,
            0.563,
            0.982
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        }
      ]
    }
